Experiment 10: Observing Virtual Memory Addresses in C
Model Institute of Engineering & Technology (Autonomous) | Course: COM-312 Operating System Lab
A comprehensive laboratory technical manual investigating the architecture of Process Virtual Address Spaces, pointer semantics in the C programming language, the impact of Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR), and the hardware Memory Management Unit (MMU) mechanisms that prevent physical memory collisions across concurrent processes.
1. Aim & Objectives
Aim:To write, compile, and execute a C program that prints the memory address of a local variable inside an infinite loop (while(1)), run 3 to 4 concurrent process instances simultaneously in the background, and analyze the resulting addresses under active and disabled Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR).
The key pedagogical objectives of this laboratory experiment are:
• Objective 1: Demystify C Pointers: Prove that memory addresses printed by C programs are virtual addresses assigned by the operating system kernel, rather than raw physical DRAM chips.
• Objective 2: Process Concurrency: Spawn multiple independent processes concurrently using the shell background operator (&) and monitor active execution states using pgrep.
• Objective 3: ASLR Exploration: Analyze modern kernel security defenses by observing how Address Space Layout Randomization introduces entropy into stack frame base addresses.
• Objective 4: Static Address Reproduction: Disable ASLR using setarch $(uname -m) -R and observe 100% identical virtual pointer values across completely independent running processes.
• Objective 5: Hardware MMU & Page Tables: Master the architectural mechanism by which the CPU Memory Management Unit (MMU) and per-process page tables map identical virtual addresses to isolated physical DRAM frames.
2. Theoretical Foundation & Core Concepts
2.1 What is a Virtual Address Space?
In modern multiprogrammed operating systems like Linux, user applications do not access physical RAM directly. Instead, every process is provided with an isolated, contiguous abstraction called the Virtual Address Space. For a 64-bit architecture (x86_64), each process perceives a vast address space spanning up to 2^48 bytes (256 Terabytes of user-space virtual memory). This virtual address space contains dedicated segments for program code (.text), initialized data (.data), uninitialized data (.bss), dynamic heap memory, shared libraries (.so), and the execution stack.
Because this address space is purely virtual, two completely unrelated processes can allocate variables at the exact same numeric virtual memory address without interfering with each other in any way. The operating system kernel enforces strict memory isolation boundaries between processes.
2.2 Virtual Addresses vs. Physical DRAM Addresses
A pointer in C (such as &var) stores a virtual memory address. When the program executes a memory read or write instruction (e.g. mov [rax], 42), the CPU does not send that address straight to the memory bus. Instead, the CPU's internal Memory Management Unit (MMU) intercepts the virtual address, traverses the active process's Page Table hierarchy, and translates the Virtual Page Number (VPN) into a Physical Frame Number (PFN) in real DRAM.
Core Architectural Rule: Under no circumstances does standard user-space C code ever expose or manipulate raw physical RAM addresses. Pointers in Linux C programs are always virtual memory abstractions managed by the kernel and MMU.


2.3 Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR)
Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR) is an operating system security feature designed to prevent memory corruption and code-injection exploits, such as Return-to-libc and Stack Buffer Overflow attacks. If the memory addresses of stack variables, functions, and libraries were static and predictable, an attacker could construct malicious input that reliably redirects execution flow to a known memory payload.
With ASLR enabled (the default state in all modern Linux distributions), the kernel randomizes the starting base address of the stack, heap, and shared memory mappings each time a program is launched. Consequently, consecutive executions of the same program display varying middle address bits, even though they remain inside the high user-space virtual range (0x7ff...).
3. C Program Source Code (virtual_addr.c)
The experiment utilizes a concise C program designed to inspect the virtual address of a stack variable and hold the process active indefinitely in RAM:
/*
 * Filename: virtual_addr.c
 * Description: Demonstrating process virtual address space isolation in Linux.
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    // Allocate an integer variable on the process stack frame
    int var = 42;

    // Print the Process ID (PID) and the hexadecimal address of 'var'
    printf("PID: %d | Address: %p\n", getpid(), (void*)&var);
    fflush(stdout);

    // Infinite loop keeps the process active so memory remains allocated
    while (1) {
        sleep(1); // Yield CPU cycles each second to avoid 100% core usage
    }

    return 0;
}


