6. In-Depth Observation & Empirical Analysis
6.1 Analysis of ASLR Active Execution (Observation A)
Under standard Linux kernel execution, the printed addresses across the four concurrent processes were:
• PID 5101: 0x7ffd8a3e41ac
• PID 5102: 0x7ffc1b9028dc
• PID 5103: 0x7ffe44d1953c
• PID 5104: 0x7ffdf012b76c
Structural Properties:Notice two crucial structural properties in these addresses:
• 1. High-Order Consistency: High-Order Bits (0x7ff...): All four pointers begin with 0x7ff, confirming that all four variables reside in user-space stack memory near the top of the 48-bit canonical virtual address range.
• 2. Stack Entropy: Intermediate Randomization: The middle hexadecimal characters (d8a, 1b9, e44, df0) vary between executions. The Linux kernel randomizes the stack base page offset upon execve(), ensuring attackers cannot predict pointer locations.
6.2 Analysis of ASLR Disabled Execution (Observation B)
When ASLR was disabled via setarch $(uname -m) -R, the output across all four live processes changed dramatically:
• PID 5210: 0x7fffffffde4c
• PID 5211: 0x7fffffffde4c
• PID 5212: 0x7fffffffde4c
• PID 5213: 0x7fffffffde4c
Because randomization was disabled and all four processes were built from identical binary ELF headers with identical environment sizes, each process initialized its stack at the exact same virtual offset: 0x7fffffffde4c. This proves beyond doubt that C memory addresses are virtual memory, not physical DRAM! If 0x7fffffffde4c were a physical memory cell, Process 2, 3, and 4 would have overwritten Process 1's memory immediately.
7. The Architectural Mechanism: Why Processes Never Collide
The reason four simultaneous processes can print and use the exact same virtual address (0x7fffffffde4c) without colliding is the dual-layer coordination between the Linux Kernel and the CPU Hardware Memory Management Unit (MMU):
7.1 Per-Process Page Table Isolation
The operating system maintains a completely separate, dedicated Page Table hierarchy (PGD, P4D, PUD, PMD, PTE on x86_64) for every active process. When Process 5210 accesses 0x7fffffffde4c, the MMU consults Page Table 5210. When Process 5211 accesses 0x7fffffffde4c, the MMU consults Page Table 5211.
7.2 The Hardware CR3 Register
When the Linux CPU scheduler performs a context switch from PID 5210 to PID 5211, it writes the physical base address of PID 5211's Page Directory into the CPU's CR3 control register. In a few clock cycles, the CPU's entire address translation world switches over. The identical virtual address points to completely different physical hardware:
Process 1 [PID 5210]: Virtual 0x7fffffffde4c  ==>  [Page Table 5210]  ==>  Physical DRAM Frame 0x0A2B000
Process 2 [PID 5211]: Virtual 0x7fffffffde4c  ==>  [Page Table 5211]  ==>  Physical DRAM Frame 0x1F4C000
Process 3 [PID 5212]: Virtual 0x7fffffffde4c  ==>  [Page Table 5212]  ==>  Physical DRAM Frame 0x2E8D000
Process 4 [PID 5213]: Virtual 0x7fffffffde4c  ==>  [Page Table 5213]  ==>  Physical DRAM Frame 0x3A01000


7.3 Complete Hardware Protection
Each physical DRAM frame is mapped exclusively into one process's address space. Process 5210 cannot read or modify Physical Frame 0x1F4C000 because that physical page simply does not exist inside Process 5210's page table. Any unauthorized memory access results in a hardware-generated Page Fault (Segmentation Fault, SIGSEGV).
8. Quick Summary for the Whiteboard
• 1. Virtual != Physical: Pointers in C are Virtual Memory Addresses: They do not represent physical hardware memory pins or DRAM chip addresses.
• 2. ASLR Role: ASLR is an OS Security Defense: It randomizes stack and heap base addresses to protect against buffer overflow exploits.
• 3. Disabling ASLR: setarch $(uname -m) -R disables ASLR: It produces static, predictable virtual memory layouts for testing and debugging.
• 4. Memory Protection: The Hardware MMU translates addresses on the fly: Per-process page tables guarantee complete isolation, even when virtual addresses are 100% identical.
