10. Result
The C program virtual_addr.c was successfully developed, compiled, and executed across multiple concurrent background instances under Linux. The experimental results conclusively demonstrated that C pointers represent virtual memory addresses rather than physical memory cells. When ASLR is active, the Linux kernel introduces randomized stack offsets to protect against memory exploits; when ASLR is disabled, identical virtual pointer values (0x7fffffffde4c) are reproduced across all concurrent processes. The experiment successfully verified that the operating system kernel and hardware Memory Management Unit (MMU) enforce complete physical memory isolation across concurrent processes through per-process page table translation.
